 Note that
\begin{align*}
\sum_{1 \le i < j \le 100} a_i a_j &= \frac{1}{2} \left[ (a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + \dots + a_{100})^2 - (a_1^2 + a_2^2 + a_3^2 + \dots + a_{100}^2) \right] \\
&= \frac{(a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + \dots + a_{100})^2 - 100}{2},
\end{align*}which is minimized when $a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + \dots + a_{100} = 2$ (or $a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + \dots + a_{100} = -2,$ but since $99 - 2 = 3 - (-2)$, we can assume $a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + \dots + a_{100} = 2$ for convenience).  Each $a_i$ is 1 or $-1$, so $a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + \dots + a_{100}$ takes along all odd integer values from $200$ to $-200$.  Therefore, both $a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + \dots + a_{100} = 2$ and $a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + \dots + a_{100} = -2$ are minimally achievable.

To make $a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + \dots + a_{100} = 2$, we need exactly 51 of the $a_i$ to be 1.  With 51 of them being 1, there are $\binom{100}{2} - \binom{51}{2} = \boxed{2450}$ values of $j - i$ that equal 3.

